// Practice quiz: Monolith vs microservices: a database per service and the trade-offs
// Unreviewed first draft, 2026-11-04. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: containers-cicd/microservices-and-ci-cd/monolith-vs-microservices

::o1 hotfix::A one-line fix in the invoicing module of a monolith must go live. What gets released? {
=The whole application#Correct. One deployable unit means everything ships together.
~Only the invoicing module#Not quite. A monolith has no separately deployable modules.
~Only the changed class#Not quite. The unit of release is the application, not a class.
~Nothing until the next scheduled sprint#Not quite. Timing is a team decision; the unit of release is still the whole application.
}

::o1 scale::Image resizing is the only busy part of a system. With it as a separate service, what can you scale? {
~The whole system, as a single unit#Not quite. That describes scaling a monolith.
=Just the image-resizing service#Correct. Separate services can run more copies independently.
~Only its database#Not quite. More service copies are the first lever here, and its database is not the busy part.
~Nothing, because services cannot be scaled#Not quite. Running more copies is exactly how services scale.
}

::o1 isolation::When does a crashed search service leave the rest of a microservices system working? {
~Always, because services are separate#Not quite. A caller that waits forever can still hang.
~Only if every service shares one database#Not quite. Sharing a database adds coupling and does nothing for this.
=When its callers handle the failed call instead of hanging on it#Correct. Isolation depends on how upstream services deal with faults.
~Never, because a failure always cascades#Not quite. Well-handled failed calls stop it spreading.
}

::o2 shared table::The billing service wants customer emails, which the accounts service stores. Which approach follows database per service? {
~Billing runs SELECT on the accounts tables#Not quite. That makes the accounts schema a contract with billing.
~Billing connects with the accounts database password#Not quite. Sharing credentials is still direct table access.
~Accounts copies its tables into a schema both services write to#Not quite. Shared writable tables keep the coupling.
=Billing calls an accounts API#Correct. The owner exposes the data through its API.
}

::o2 why::Why must one service not read another service's tables directly? {
=Any schema change would have to be coordinated with every reader#Correct. Private tables are what let an owner change freely.
~Databases allow only one connection#Not quite. Databases accept many connections.
~SQL cannot cross a network#Not quite. It can; the issue is coupling.
~It is always slower than an API call#Not quite. Speed is not the reason.
}

::o3 cost::An order must take payment and reserve stock, and those live in two services with private databases. Which new cost appears? {
~The code can no longer be tested#Not quite. Each service can still be tested.
=One database transaction cannot cover both, so consistency needs extra design#Correct. This is the consistency cost across services.
~HTTP cannot carry order data#Not quite. It can.
~Each service must use a different language#Not quite. Nothing requires that.
}
